  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Timor-Leste, the Farming for Prosperity Program (TOMAK) has improved adaptation to climate change and resilience to disasters through training and support for 2161 participants representing an estimated 9,707 family members. Farmers supported by TOMAK are increasingly adopting Climate-Smart Agriculture (CSA) with 45% using stress-tolerant varieties of crops and livestock, 84% of farmers using at least one water management tool and 77% of farmers reporting water sufficiency during planting due to support for water spring protection supports. Techniques such as intercropping, crop rotation, and diversification (averaging 13.9 crops per household) are strengthening soil fertility, food security, and nutrition while harmful practices like land burning have declined. Terracing is 94% complete on sloped farmland, notably in Bobonaro, reducing erosion and increasing arable land. Challenges remain, especially for people with disabilities, due to limited access to tools and knowledge. To address this, TOMAK has provided key inputs, including organic fertilizers, climate-resilient seeds, and water management tools, through a 50% cost-share to over 2,000 families.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Timor-Leste, the Farming for Prosperity Program (TOMAK) has supported 2161 smallholder farmers (whose households total 9,707 people) with training and demonstrations facilitated through Farmer Field Schools, improved access to agricultural inputs and access to better financial management through savings and loans groups. 
&#x200d;
As a result, TOMAK-supported farmers are demonstrating stronger resilience to seasonal food insecurity, reporting significantly fewer incidences of food shortages (50% less when compared with non-supported farmers).  This improvement reflects multiple, reinforcing gains. Increased crops and livestock production, especially the rising ownership of pigs and chickens, has boosted household food availability. Access to finance through Savings and Loans Groups (SLGs) is enabling farmers to invest in tools, inputs, and practices that improve productivity, but also cope during lean periods. Adoption of Good Agricultural Practices (GAPs), coupled with increased crop diversification, is helping farmers manage seasonal risks more effectively.
&#x200d;
In Timor-Leste&#x2019;s climate-sensitive agricultural calendar, these shifts are significant. By supporting smarter planning, better production, and improved access to resources, TOMAK is helping reduce vulnerability to seasonal shocks and contributing to more stable, food-secure rural livelihoods.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Disability equity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">TOMAK and Ra&#x2019;es Hadomi Timor Oan (RHTO) co-developed the disability inclusion training manual which has become a key resource for building staff capacity and promoting inclusive practices. RHTO regularly uses the manual to support their training for government agencies and civil society organizations. This partnership has had the dual benefit of building the capacity of RHTO staff in resource development and delivery of effective training and strengthening the partnership with TOMAK to ensure more people with disabilities have access to climate-smart agriculture (CSA) technologies and services. With TOMAK support, RHTO also contributed to a high-profile event on Inclusive Labour-Saving Technologies to raise visibility of disability inclusion across sectors. RHTO also contributed to the revised National Disability Action Plan, and supported TOMAK to secure the Ministry of Agriculture Livestock, Farming and Fisheries (MALFF) involvement.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved Governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">TOMAK has played a key role in supporting the Ministry of Agriculture, Livestock, Fisheries and Forestry (MALFF) to strengthen inclusion in national policy frameworks. With technical support from TOMAK, MALFF&#x2019;s revision of the national agricultural extension manual was formally validated by the Minister. The manual marks a critical step toward strengthening and standardising extension services across Timor-Leste, offering practical guidance to frontline extension workers to improve the consistency, quality, and inclusiveness of farmer support. TOMAK identified that women were not being adequately served by existing extension systems, with many Agricultural Extension Workers (AEWs) not recognising women as farmers. In response, TOMAK included AEWs in targeted GEDSI-related training to improve inclusion and worked to ensure that the revised manual promotes inclusive, gender-responsive extension practices.
&#x200d;
TOMAK also collaborated with MALFF and SSE (Secretary of State for Equality), to develop the Maubisse Declaration Phase Three implementation plan and monitoring framework. TOMAK provided technical support to ensure the plan was inclusive by integrating gender, youth, and disability considerations. To further support implementation, TOMAK trained key MALFF staff and Agricultural Extension Workers (AEWs) in disability inclusion across Baucau, Viqueque, and Bobonaro.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Food security</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">TOMAK is improving climate resilient agriculture practices for improved food security through the development of climate smart-practices training guides used by agriculture extension workers and TOMAK staff to directly train 1,038 (M:274; F:764; PWD: 36; Youth:165) small holder farmers in the reporting period. Imparting knowledge on improved nutrition and health standards is delivered through TOMAK&#x2019;s nutrition-sensitive-agriculture training and in partnership with local health centres. Support to particularly vulnerable groups included training on and access to hydroponic systems tailored for people with disabilities. TOMAK&#x2019;s support to accessing markets is increasing food availability while small-holder farmers are able to increase their economic resilience through their agriculture activities. In the reporting period TOMAK facilitated sales of USD80,000 (AUD120,000). Overall, TOMAK has supported 4,872 smallholder farmers between July 2023 and June 2024. (male: 2,288; female: 2,584; youth: 927; and people with a disability: 59)</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Climate adaptation, mitigation, environment and biodiversity</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">TOMAK is working with communities to increase their resilience to disasters by implementing improved natural resource management of water sources, including water harvesting and storage to offset water scarcity challenges. Additionally, through training on sloping-agriculture-land-technology (SALT) and agroforestry, TOMAK works with communities to reduce instances of high wind damage, landslides and erosion while increasing ground water holding capacity to reduce flash flooding. During the reporting period a total of 1,019 small-holder farmers were trained on climate resilient practices. This includes, 268 men, 751 women, 34 persons with disability, and 162 youth. Of this 260 are actively implementing these climate resilient practices on their land.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved governance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">With TOMAK support, Ministry of Agriculture, Livestock, Forestry and Fishery (MALFF) provided consolidated input into the draft of the Coordinated Border Management National Strategy which has been submitted to the Border Management Committee&apos;s Secretariat. The consolidated input includes the integration of the gender-responsive, child-sensitive, disability-sensitive and culturally sensitive approaches, as well as upholding the principle of non-discrimination in the vision statement of the CBM National Strategy</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2023-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2024-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
